    {
      "id": "openvoicecs-v0.1.0-unobservable-required-events",
      "type": "scoring_changed",
      "date": "2026-08-09",
      "summary": "Required events that no observable behaviour can emit are no longer charged against the agent. Derivation produces 20 event names; the corpus declares 53 required ones, and provider adapters give a model no events channel, so across 8877 recorded trials no event ever came from an agent rather than from derivation. An agent reproducing the oracle exactly scored 198 of 220 scenarios, and 2 of the 19 multi-turn ones, purely for steps it had no way to demonstrate; it now scores 219 and 19. The same rule applies to speaker gates, one of whose two instances keys on an unemittable event. Verification ordering is also enforced: check_authentication_integrity asked only whether a verification event existed anywhere in the trace, not whether it preceded the protected call. The oracle is unchanged at 100.00 because it reports its own events; the no-op baseline moves 24.89 to 24.92.",
      "compatibility": "scoring_affecting"
    },
    {
      "id": "openvoicecs-v0.1.0-state-grounded-claims",
      "type": "scoring_changed",
      "date": "2026-08-09",
      "summary": "factual_grounding verifies claims against replayed state instead of matching literal phrases. It previously asked whether particular wording appeared and never whether what the agent said was true: an invented money figure appended to an otherwise correct trace passed in 218 of 218 fabricated cases, while a correct fact restated in different words failed in 153 of 153. Across the published sweep, 265 recorded trials reached the correct final state, said the fee was waived in their own words, and were marked ungrounded; a further 112 were genuine omissions the old check caught correctly. A required claim now counts when its literals match, or when the expected state holds and either changed or was written by a tool the agent called. Money figures stated in the reply are checked against the sandbox. This changes leaderboard order: re-scoring 14 recorded runs moves 11 of them, the top two holding and third place swapping.",
      "compatibility": "scoring_affecting"
    },
    {
      "id": "openvoicecs-v0.1.0-privacy-checks-non-vacuous",
      "type": "scoring_changed",
      "date": "2026-08-09",
      "summary": "Two privacy checks that no behaviour could fail now require evidence. pii_minimization is a required event in all 220 scenarios and was derived from the absence of a forbidden disclosure, so a trace with no tool calls and no refusal collected it; it is now derived only when the agent called a tool or refused, refusing included because declining is the correct action in the adversarial scenarios. And _is_negated_disclosure_context read a fixed 80-character window, so 'I cannot share your full SSN, it is 123-45-6789' scored clean in all 219 scenarios declaring a disclosure pattern; it now reads only the clause holding the match. Forbidden tool patterns are also matchable again: generated_arguments no longer substitutes the declared value before the forbidden check runs, which had left 153 of 229 patterns unmatchable, while expected patterns keep the substitution so a model is still not charged for a value it cannot know. The oracle stays at 100.00 and real models' privacy scores are unchanged; the no-op baseline falls from 24.92 to 7.68. The reference baseline manifest is deliberately left at its pre-change values: regenerating the no-op reports rewrites roughly 30,000 lines of generated JSON, which buries the reviewable diff. Run `make baselines && make audit && make datasheet` to bring them forward.",
      "compatibility": "scoring_affecting"
    }
  ],
  "errata": [
    {
      "id": "openvoicecs-erratum-forbidden-patterns-unreachable",
      "summary": "153 of 229 forbidden tool-call patterns could not be triggered by any agent. A pattern is forbidden because of one argument value, and generated_arguments was applied with update() before the forbidden check ran, so the declared value replaced what the model sent and the forbidden call became the legitimate one. Resolved by giving the two halves of check_tool_calls different views of the same calls: expected patterns keep the substitution, because 82.7% of those slots are omitted and 16.4% carry a guess in recorded runs and charging them again would reintroduce the unknowable-argument defect, while forbidden patterns are matched against what the model submitted. All 229 patterns are now matchable; across 2063 recorded real trials the number of forbidden matches is unchanged at zero, so this restores reachability without penalising any real model.",
      "status": "resolved",
      "severity": "high"
    },
